{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% import "_macros.html.j2" as m with context %}
{% block title %}Submitting a tool — Capsid Hub{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<header class="page-head">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="{{ root }}index.html">Capsid Hub</a> / Guides / Submitting a tool</p>
  <h1>Submitting a tool</h1>
  <p class="lede">A contribution is a recipe (one tool version) or a stack (several tools in one image), with its
    lockfile. After review and merge, the image is built, tested, signed and published to
    <code>{{ REGISTRY }}</code> without further action from you.</p>
</header>

<nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#routes">Three routes</a></li>
    <li><a href="#prerequisites">Prerequisites</a></li>
    <li><a href="#steps">Step by step</a></li>
    <li><a href="#anatomy">Anatomy of a recipe</a></li>
    <li><a href="#build-types">Choosing a build type</a></li>
    <li><a href="#tests">Writing the test</a></li>
    <li><a href="#stacks">Stacks</a></li>
    <li><a href="#review">Review</a></li>
    <li><a href="#after">After the merge</a></li>
    <li><a href="#updates">Updating a tool</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<section class="numbered" id="routes">
  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>Three routes</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>You need</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Pull request</th><td>Git, the Capsid CLI, Docker or Podman</td>
          <td>You write, lock and build the recipe locally; the audit workflow repeats lint, build and test. Fastest to merge.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Issue form</th><td>a GitHub account and a recipe</td>
          <td>Paste the recipe into the <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/issues/new?template=new_recipe.yml">New recipe</a> form. It is linted,
            built and tested in isolation; if that passes, a pull request is opened and a maintainer adds the lockfile before merging.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Request</th><td>a GitHub account</td>
          <td>Tools released on Bioconda are proposed every Friday by the upstream robot. For anything else, open an
            <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/issues">issue</a> naming the tool and version.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Check the <a href="{{ root }}catalogue/index.html">catalogue</a> and the
      <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/pulls">open pull requests</a> first: the version may already exist or be under review.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="prerequisites">
  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Prerequisites</h2>
  <div class="body">
{{ m.code("# Capsid CLI (Python 3.12+), installed with uv\nuv tool install git+" ~ ENGINE_URL ~ ".git\ncapsid --help\n\n# A container engine: Docker with BuildKit, or Podman\ndocker buildx version", "sh") }}
    <p>The lock step runs the solvers (pixi, uv) inside pinned container images, so nothing else needs to be
      installed on the host. Editors validate recipes as you type with the JSON Schema in
      <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/tree/main/schema"><code>schema/</code></a>; VS Code picks it up from the repository settings.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="steps">
  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>Step by step</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><h3>Fork and branch</h3>
{{ m.code("gh repo fork " ~ HUB_REPO ~ " --clone && cd capsid-hub\ngit switch -c add-<tool>-<version>", "sh") }}</li>
      <li><h3>Generate the recipe</h3>
        <p><code>capsid init</code> reads the upstream metadata and writes <code>recipes/&lt;tool&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/recipe.yaml</code>.
          Writing it by hand is equally valid.</p>
{{ m.code("capsid init bioconda:<tool>@<version>          # package on Bioconda / conda-forge\ncapsid init pypi:<tool>@<version>              # pure Python package\ncapsid init github:<owner>/<repo> -v <version>  # source release", "sh") }}</li>
      <li><h3>Review it</h3>
        <p>Generated recipes are a starting point. Check the description, the SPDX licence, the platforms and above all the
          test (see <a href="#tests">§6</a>).</p></li>
      <li><h3>Lock the closure</h3>
{{ m.code("capsid lock recipes/<tool>/<version>/recipe.yaml", "sh") }}
        <p>Writes <code>pixi.lock</code> or <code>requirements.lock</code> next to the recipe, for every platform it lists.
          Source and binary recipes have no lockfile; their archives are pinned by <code>sha256</code> in the recipe.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Lint, build and test</h3>
{{ m.code("capsid lint recipes/<tool>/<version>/recipe.yaml --catalog catalog.json\ncapsid build recipes/<tool>/<version>/recipe.yaml      # runs the test after building", "sh") }}
        <p>The linter's errors block the merge; warnings need a reason in the pull request.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Submit</h3>
{{ m.code("git add recipes/<tool>/<version>/\ngit commit -m \"feat(recipe): add <tool> <version>\"\ngit push -u origin add-<tool>-<version>\ngh pr create --repo " ~ HUB_REPO, "sh") }}
        <p><code>capsid submit recipes/&lt;tool&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/recipe.yaml</code> lints the recipe and then does the fork,
          branch, commit, push and pull request in one step with the GitHub CLI; with <code>--web</code> it opens a pre-filled
          issue instead.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="anatomy">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>Anatomy of a recipe</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <div class="anatomy">
{{ m.code("name: samtools\nversion: '1.25'\ndescription: Tools for dealing with SAM, BAM and CRAM files\nhomepage: https://github.com/samtools/samtools\nlicense: MIT\ncategories: [genomics, alignment]\nplatforms: [linux/amd64, linux/arm64]\nbuild:\n  type: pixi-conda\n  conda_packages: [samtools==1.25]\n  channels: [conda-forge, bioconda]\ntest:\n  command: samtools --version\n  expected_output: samtools 1.25", "yaml") }}
      <table class="booktabs compact">
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">name, version</td><td>Image name and tag; must equal the directory names. Lowercase name.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">description</td><td>One line, at least 10 characters.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">homepage, license</td><td>Upstream project and an SPDX identifier or expression that allows redistribution.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">doi, edam_topics, edam_operations</td><td>Optional; carried into the RO-Crate metadata and shown on the tool page.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">platforms</td><td><code>linux/amd64</code>, <code>linux/arm64</code>: only those where the tool builds and works.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.type</td><td>Where the software comes from; see <a href="#build-types">§5</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.conda_packages, channels</td><td>Pinned with <code>==</code> (pixi-conda).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.package, python_version</td><td>PyPI requirement pinned with <code>==</code> (uv-pypi).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.source, sha256</td><td>Source archive and its checksum (autotools, cmake, make, rust-cargo).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.binary_url_amd64, sha256_amd64, …</td><td>Pre-built archive per architecture and checksum (binary).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">build.dependencies, build_commands</td><td>Debian packages for build and run; extra shell steps, reviewed line by line.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">test.*</td><td>The acceptance test; see <a href="#tests">§6</a>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/recipe-reference.md">Complete recipe reference →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="build-types">
  <h2><span class="secno">5</span>Choosing a build type</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Prefer, in this order:</p>
    <ol class="propositions">
      <li><strong><code>pixi-conda</code></strong> when the tool is on Bioconda or conda-forge. Pre-built for both
        architectures, lockable, no compilation.</li>
      <li><strong><code>uv-pypi</code></strong> for pure Python tools on PyPI.</li>
      <li><strong>A source build</strong> (<code>autotools</code>, <code>make</code>, <code>cmake</code>, <code>rust-cargo</code>)
        from a release archive pinned by checksum.</li>
      <li><strong><code>binary</code></strong> only when nothing else exists: the build cannot be inspected.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="tests">
  <h2><span class="secno">6</span>Writing the test</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>The test runs after every build, again on native amd64 and arm64 machines after publication, and on users'
      clusters with <code>capsid test</code>. It should prove that the tool works, not only that a file exists.
      Trivial commands (<code>true</code>, <code>exit 0</code>, <code>echo</code>) are rejected by the linter.</p>
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Minimum</td><td><code>command: samtools --version</code> with <code>expected_output: samtools 1.25</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Better</td><td>a small computation on inline input (<code>test.files</code>, written to <code>/test</code>) with known output</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best</td><td>the above plus <code>output_file</code> and <code>expected_checksum</code>, and <code>random_seed</code> for stochastic tools</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
{{ m.code("test:\n  files:\n    in.sam: |\n      @SQ\tSN:chr1\tLN:100\n      r1\t0\tchr1\t1\t60\t4M\t*\t0\t0\tACGT\tIIII\n  command: samtools view -c in.sam\n  expected_output: \"1\"", "yaml") }}
    <p>Tools that print usage and exit non-zero without arguments can set <code>expected_exit_code</code>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="stacks">
  <h2><span class="secno">7</span>Stacks</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>A stack combines recipes already in the hub. Its components must be <code>pixi-conda</code> or
      <code>uv-pypi</code>, share at least one platform and be compatible in one environment, because they are solved together.</p>
{{ m.code("# stacks/<stack-name>.yaml\nname: <stack-name>\nversion: 1.0.0\ndescription: What the tools are used for together\ncomponents:\n  - recipe: samtools\n    version: \"1.25\"\n  - recipe: bcftools\n    version: \"1.25\"\ntest:\n  command: samtools --version && bcftools --version", "yaml") }}
{{ m.code("capsid lock stacks/<stack-name>.yaml\ncapsid lint stacks/<stack-name>.yaml\ncapsid build stacks/<stack-name>.yaml\ngit add stacks/<stack-name>.*", "sh") }}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="review">
  <h2><span class="secno">8</span>Review</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>The pull request is checked automatically, with read-only permissions: the schema is validated,
      <code>capsid lint</code> runs, and every changed recipe and stack is built and tested. A maintainer then checks:</p>
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Meaningful test</th><td>it runs the tool and checks a result</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Everything pinned</th><td>packages with <code>==</code>, lockfile committed, <code>sha256</code> for every download</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Accurate metadata</th><td>description, homepage, a redistributable SPDX licence</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Real platforms</th><td><code>linux/arm64</code> only if the tool works there</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Readable build steps</th><td>no <code>curl … | sh</code>, <code>sudo</code>, <code>chmod 777</code>, or disabled TLS checks (also lint errors)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Common lint errors: <code>E003</code>/<code>E004</code> name or version differs from the directory;
      <code>E011</code> download without checksum; <code>E016</code> lockfile out of date (rerun <code>capsid lock</code>);
      <code>E017</code> lockfile lacks a listed platform; <code>E030</code> trivial test.
      <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/docs/security.md#recipe-linter">All rules →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="after">
  <h2><span class="secno">9</span>After the merge</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <ol class="steps compact">
      <li><code>publish-release</code> builds the image for each listed platform and runs the test.</li>
      <li>It pushes the multi-architecture tag, signs the digest with Cosign and attaches the SBOM and SLSA provenance.</li>
      <li>Native amd64 and arm64 runners pull the signed digest, verify the signature and run the test again.</li>
      <li>The catalogue and this site are regenerated; the tool gets its own pages.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The whole run usually takes a few minutes per image; its log is public in the repository's
      <a href="{{ HUB_URL }}/actions">Actions</a> tab.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="updates">
  <h2><span class="secno">10</span>Updating a tool</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>How</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>New upstream version</td><td>new directory <code>recipes/&lt;tool&gt;/&lt;new-version&gt;/</code></td><td>new tag; earlier versions stay published</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fix to a published version (test, metadata, platform)</td><td>edit in place; explain why in the pull request</td><td>same tag, new digest (lint warning <code>W040</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Security fix in a dependency</td><td>rerun <code>capsid lock</code> and commit the lockfile</td><td>same tag, new digest</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>To change the engine itself (generated Dockerfiles, CLI), contribute to
      <a href="{{ ENGINE_URL }}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">Capsid</a> instead.</p>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
